<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-history-of-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The history of science asks <strong>how humans learned to learn about nature reliably</strong>
    — a question with a surprisingly short answer: mostly since 1600. What changed wasn't
    intelligence (Aristotle was no fool) but method and institutions: experiments, mathematics,
    publishing, and the social contract that lets a rival check your work. The story explains
    both why science works and why it took civilisations so long to build it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four transformations, from philosophy to big science" widget="equationStep" config={sciCfg} />

  <h2>Themes the history teaches</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scientific revolutions are real but gradual:</strong> Copernicus (1543) was "just" a better calendar calculation; a century of resistance, refinement and Newton's synthesis made heliocentrism irresistible. Kuhn's "paradigm shifts" (1962) describe the pattern — with historians arguing about the details.</li>
    <li><strong>Instruments create discoveries:</strong> the telescope (1608) made Galileo's career; the microscope opened a hidden world; the particle accelerator built a century of physics. Funding and glassware are part of the story of truth.</li>
    <li><strong>Religion's role was complicated:</strong> the Church funded astronomy (calendars), prosecuted Galileo, and many key figures were clergy. The "warfare" narrative (Draper, 1874) is a Victorian simplification historians have largely retired.</li>
    <li><strong>Who gets counted:</strong> Rosalind Franklin's data in the double helix, women computers at NASA, non-Western science (the Islamic Golden Age lesson, Chinese inventions) — the field keeps widening its frame.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962); Wootton, The Invention of Science (2015); Shapin, The Scientific Revolution.</p>
</TopicPage>
